1
00:08:49,411 --> 00:08:55,411
Playing for Time is a drama, music, history film directed by Daniel Mann.

2
00:17:38,823 --> 00:17:44,823
When a Jewish songstress is plucked from the stage and sent to Auschwitz, she and other musicians find themselves assigned to
a terrible task—using their talents to soothe fellow prisoners who are sentenced to die in the gas chambers.

3
00:26:28,235 --> 00:26:34,235
Fania Fenelon, played by Vanessa Redgrave.

4
00:35:17,647 --> 00:35:23,647
Alma Rose, played by Jane Alexander.

5
00:44:07,058 --> 00:44:13,058
Mala, played by Maud Adams.

6
00:52:56,470 --> 00:53:02,470
Olga, played by Christine Baranski.

7
01:01:45,882 --> 01:01:51,882
Etalina, played by Robin Bartlett.

8
01:10:35,294 --> 01:10:41,294
Elzvieta, played by Marisa Berenson.

9
01:19:24,705 --> 01:19:30,705
Playing for Time is a 1980 CBS television film, written by Arthur Miller and based on
musician Fania Fénelon's autobiography The Musicians of Auschwitz.

10
01:28:14,117 --> 01:28:20,117
Vanessa Redgrave stars as Fénelon.

11
01:37:03,529 --> 01:37:09,529
Playing for Time was based on Fénelon's experience as a female prisoner in the Auschwitz concentration camp, where
she and a group of classical musicians were spared in return for performing music for their captors.

12
01:45:52,941 --> 01:45:58,941
The film was later adapted as a play by Miller.

13
01:54:42,352 --> 01:54:48,352
This was the last film of director Daniel Mann.

14
02:03:31,764 --> 02:03:37,764
Parts of the film were directed by Joseph Sargent, but
only Mann was credited as director.

15
02:12:21,176 --> 02:12:27,176
The film explores themes of concentration camp, world war ii, auschwitz-birkenau
concentration camp, orchestra, based on memoir or autobiography.

16
02:21:10,588 --> 02:21:16,588
The tone is emotional.
